mdmota@yahoo.com.au (Mark) wrote

> Had a few charecters complete the ascencion in nethack, so was looking
> for something new, and Ive heard a bit about slashem, just wondering
> how much harder it was then nethack, especially getting used to all
> the new monsters and items?

Harder in some ways, easier in others. Getting used to the new stuff
is fairly easy, broadly speaking, although of course it'll get you 
killed repeatedly (don't eat an asphynx corpse). The extra equipment
is balanced by more shops and shopkeeper services, and more midgame
wishes. The new monsters mostly just make for tougher genocide choices, 
and a few surprises.

The early game is slightly easier than Nethack's, IMO. Character 
techniques give more options to the hero, and a broader pool of
equipment for those who get random stuff means that they start less
often with crappy or cursed items (but also less often with extremely
favorable ones).

The middle and late games tend to be rougher, due mainly to bosses 
along the way, although the plane of air is easier on you. Also an
ascension kit is harder to choose and build, because there's more
useful intrinsics to choose from and plan around. The endgame is
far less tedious, though.

Monks in SLASH'EM are considerably stronger than their Nethack 
counterparts, and are probably the easiest class to play. The new
spellcasters start with worse equipment than the wizard but better
pets, and are comparable in difficulty in my opinion. Yeomen are
combat-wombats, comparable to the knight in difficulty but without
the goofy code of conduct. Undead Slayers are easier than rogues 
but not so easy as straight-combat types.

Of the new races, the doppelganger is relatively easy - it gets
a good natural AC, polymorph ability (but not control for a while),
and good stats. It can't #twoweapon, though. Vampires are pretty
rough, and require developing a totally different playstyle. 
Lycanthropes are extremely difficult to play, at least for me.

So... about as difficult as Nethack, and not too great a learning
curve (you already know the principles, it's just a matter of
integrating more stuff and some new properties for old stuff). It's
got more variety and a far more interesting late game, though, in
my opinion.

